# Awareness rules — resurfacing the frame

> **Reminders, not gates.** Nothing here blocks anything. An agent deep in task 14 has not
> *disobeyed* the plan; it has lost sight of it, because its context is full of task 14. You do
> not fix that by denying a tool call. You fix it by putting the frame back in front of the agent
> at the moments it is most likely to have gone.

## The one rule that decides whether any of this works

**A reminder that repeats is wallpaper. A reminder that reports is news.**

Static text — the rules file pasted in again, the same banner every turn — stops being read after
the third firing. Worse, it teaches the agent that injected text is skippable, which degrades the
reminders that *do* matter.

So every reminder here is **computed from current state**: task statuses parsed from disk, the
commit log, the changelog. Then the message differs every time it fires, because the situation
differs, and it keeps being read for the same reason a status line is read and a splash screen is
not.

This also makes it cheap: one script, no model, no tokens, one source of truth feeding several
surfaces.

---

## Three registers

Firing one reminder everywhere is the mistake. Different moments carry different weight.

### 1 · Ambient — one line, constantly

```
Wave 2 · T14 search-guard · 6/20 done · 1 blocked (T09)
```

Position only. **No rules in this register** — hard rules repeated every turn become wallpaper
within an hour and take the ambient line down with them. This exists so the agent is never more
than one line away from knowing where it is.

### 2 · Situational — at the moment of relevance

Triggered by what the agent is about to do, and **non-blocking**:

> *note: this file is owned by T12 — the fragment protocol applies here*

This is how the ownership rule gets teeth without the harness ever saying no. It is never noise,
because it only fires when it is the relevant fact. A situational reminder that fires when it is
not relevant is an ambient reminder wearing a disguise, and should be demoted.

### 3 · Punctuated — the full picture, rarely, at boundaries

```
WHERE:     Wave 2 of 4 · T14 in progress
CLOSED:    T11, T12, T13 since last checkpoint
IN FLIGHT: T14 (you), T15 (migrator)
BLOCKED:   T09 — awaiting decision D4
CHANGED:   plan amended twice since kickoff → CHANGELOG
NEXT:      T16 gates on T14; wave 3 gates on all of wave 2
```

This is the institutional step-back — the thing standups and retrospectives exist to force in
human teams. It is where hard rules belong, because a boundary is when the frame is being rebuilt
anyway.

Note where **CHANGED** comes from: the amendment receipts required by principle 13. That
mechanism was built for exactly this feed.

---

## The moments

Event names below are the common ones; **confirm the injection contract for each against your
harness's hook documentation** before wiring, since what a hook may return differs per event.

| Moment | Register | Why this instant |
|---|---|---|
| Session start | punctuated | cold or warm start — orient before acting |
| **Before compaction** | **punctuated** | **the single highest-value hook — see below** |
| A worker finishes | punctuated | one unit closed; the cheapest moment to zoom out |
| Turn ends | thin check | *does the status line reflect what you just did?* |
| Every turn | ambient | position, one line |
| Before a tool call | situational | ownership, contended files, the deployed checkout |

### Why the pre-compaction moment matters most

Compaction is **literally the instant of forgetting.** Every other mechanism in this method tries
to *recover* from lost context. A reminder fired before compaction is the only one that puts the
durable state **into the thing being summarised**, so the frame survives as part of the summary
rather than being what the summary drops.

If you wire exactly one hook, wire that one.

---

## What the state script must do

One script, two modes, called from several events. Requirements are mostly about not being
harmful:

- **Never fail.** Exit zero on every path. A reminder that breaks a tool call is worse than no
  reminder at all.
- **Never be slow.** No network. No expensive history walks. It runs constantly.
- **Degrade out loud.** If there are no task files yet, say so in one line. Silence is
  indistinguishable from "everything is fine."
- **Read, never write.** It reports state; it does not maintain it. The moment a reminder starts
  editing the plan, you no longer know who moved it.
- **Stay boring.** No cleverness, no summarising, no model calls. Its whole value is that it
  reports what is literally on disk.

See [`awareness.sh.template`](awareness.sh.template) for a starting implementation.

---

## Do NOT

- Do NOT block anything from these hooks. Awareness and enforcement are different jobs; a
  reminder that can deny is an enforcement mechanism with a friendly tone, and it will be
  designed as one within a week.
- Do NOT put rules in the ambient register.
- Do NOT emit an identical message twice in a row. If the state has not changed, the ambient line
  is enough.
- Do NOT let the script write to the plan, the status files, or the changelog.
- Do NOT add a register because a moment *exists*. Every reminder competes for the same
  attention; adding one makes the others cheaper to ignore.

---

## Why this is not enforcement

It would be easy to read this file and build blocking hooks instead — they are simpler, and they
*feel* stronger.

They are not, for this problem. The failure being addressed is not disobedience, it is
**attentional narrowing**: a worker is doing task 14 correctly and has lost the shape of the plan
around it. Blocking a tool call does not restore that shape; it just stops the one action the
harness happened to be able to pattern-match, while the frame stays gone.

Enforcement has its place — file ownership, the deployed checkout, secrets — and that place is
[`_RULES.md`](_RULES.md). This file is the other half: keeping the agent oriented, so the rules
have something to be obeyed *in service of*.
